<p class=3DMsoNormal>Normal phone operation</p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal>Android provides APIs specifically intended for <b>ded=
icated/kiosk
devices</b>, where an application can restrict access to other apps and sys=
tem
features.</p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><b>iPhone/iPad<o:p></o:p></b></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal>It's more restricted.</p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal>Apple supports <b>Guided Access</b>, which can tempora=
rily
restrict a device to a single app, but a normal third-party app cannot simp=
ly
take complete control of someone's iPhone whenever it wants.</p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal>For institutional devices, Apple's <b>MDM/supervised-d=
evice</b>
capabilities can provide stronger restrictions.</p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span class=3DGramE>So</span> if your goal is:</p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><b>&quot;Any student downloads our <span class=3DGramE=
>app</span>
and we completely lock their personal phone during an exam.&quot;</b></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal>You <b>cannot reliably guarantee that on every phone</=
b>,
particularly iPhones.</p>

