<p class=3DMsoNormal><b>But here's the catch<o:p></o:p></b></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal>You can't just write:</p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span class=3DSpellE><span class=3DGramE>lockPhone</sp=
an></span><span
class=3DGramE>(</span>)</p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal>and suddenly <span class=3DGramE>control</span> the en=
tire
phone.</p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal>Android has security restrictions around this.</p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal>For strong kiosk behavior, the device generally needs =
to be
configured/managed appropriately, such as being provisioned as a dedicated =
device
or using device-management capabilities.</p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal>That's why I'd distinguish:</p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><b>Personal phone<o:p></o:p></b></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal>Student downloads your app &#8594; limited control.</p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><b>Managed/dedicated device<o:p></o:p></b></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal>Institution configures the phone &#8594; much stronger
lockdown.</p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal>Your project is interesting because you're proposing <=
b>students'
own phones</b>, so you need to design around what Android permits for that
scenario.</p>

